    "question": {
      ":question/id": "c883c312-7e30-52ad-a258-e31d542d129a",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{x y}{2}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-1-policy-2-e0ceec0efc26ec01/assets/17592186172040/plots/a20dd037e93e01fa8e60bf801654c30aca622341be052dbcbaba197b2ed6de9e.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172040/review-1-policy-2-e0ceec0efc26ec01/assets/17592186172040/plots/dcd148f6c1379304f4dd535594cf2e7a13b208459088b51d06e9fd67b015da7b.png)",
